.TH MORSEDEC-IQLIVE 1 "2026-10-16" "libmorsedec 0.2.0" "General Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
morsedec-iqlive \- decode streaming Morse I/Q from standard input
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B morsedec-iqlive
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B morsedec-iqlive
decodes interleaved complex I/Q samples from standard input. Samples are
provided as I,Q pairs, with I first, and are interpreted as
.IR I+jQ .
Reported frequencies are signed Hz offsets from the stream's 0 Hz IF center.
Positive complex rotation produces a positive offset.
.PP
The tool does not open SDR devices. Pipe or redirect a raw I/Q stream into it.
Input is processed until end of file, a termination signal, or the optional
input duration limit.
.PP
By default, messages from the selected channel are written to standard output
as text snapshots. Use
.B -a
to print all tracked channels or
.B -j
for JSON Lines snapshots, including revisions. Diagnostics are written to
standard error. An optional CSV trace records per-hop channel metrics, and
.B -T
replaces the text snapshots with a full-screen terminal view.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI -r " HZ"
Set the input sample rate in complex pairs per second. Required.
.TP
.BI -F " s16|f32"
Set the sample format. The default is
.B s16
(signed 16-bit little-endian PCM). The alternative
.B f32
is little-endian IEEE binary32.
.TP
.BI -f " HZ"
Pin decoding to a signed frequency offset from 0 Hz IF. Zero selects the IF
center. Without this option, signals are discovered automatically.
.TP
.BI -l " HZ"
Set the signed lower retained band edge. The default is -1200 Hz.
.TP
.BI -u " HZ"
Set the exclusive upper retained band edge. The default is 1200 Hz.
.TP
.BI -w " WPM"
Set the initial Morse speed estimate, from 5 through 80 WPM. The default uses
model priors.
.TP
.BI -s " N"
Stop after consuming
.I N
seconds worth of complex input samples. This limits input duration, not
wall-clock time.
.TP
.B -a
Print messages from all tracked channels instead of only the selected channel.
.TP
.B -j
Write JSON Lines message snapshots, including revisions, instead of human
readable text.
.TP
.B -T
Show the decode in a full-screen terminal view instead of writing text
snapshots to standard output. The left pane lists every tracked channel with
its signed frequency offset, estimated SNR, speed and state; the right pane
shows the decoded messages, wrapped and scrollable. See
.B TERMINAL VIEW
below. JSON Lines
.RB ( -j )
and the CSV trace
.RB ( -t )
keep working alongside the view. A build configured with
.B -Dtui=disabled
rejects this option.
.TP
.BI -t " FILE"
Write per-hop channel metrics as CSV to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B -V
Print version, copyright, and warranty information, then exit.
.TP
.B -h
Print a short usage summary, then exit.
.SH INPUT FORMAT
Input is read from standard input as headerless, interleaved I/Q values in
little-endian byte order. The
.B s16
format is signed 16-bit PCM; the
.B f32
format is IEEE 754 binary32. Each complex sample is represented by I followed
by Q, so the sample rate counts pairs rather than individual scalar values. No
resampling is performed. The format and rate must match the stream producer.
An incomplete I/Q pair at end of input is reported as an error.
.SH STREAM HANDLING
The tool retains partial reads between system calls and processes complete
decoder hops as they arrive. End of file, SIGINT, SIGTERM, or the duration limit
flushes pending decoder output before exit. Empty input is valid. A signal is
handled in the main processing loop so pending output and resources can be
drained and released normally.
.PP
On Windows the tool waits on the standard input handle instead of polling and
switches standard input to binary mode. Ctrl-C is delivered by the console
handler; SIGTERM and SIGPIPE do not exist, and a closed downstream pipe becomes
a write error rather than a signal.
.PP
The optional CSV trace includes synthetic drain time at shutdown. I/Q input has
no timestamps or discontinuity markers; dropped samples cannot be detected by
the decoder.
.SH TERMINAL VIEW
With
.BR -T ,
a two pane view is drawn on the controlling terminal. The left pane lists every
tracked channel as a signed frequency offset in Hz, estimated SNR in dB,
estimated speed in WPM and decoder state; the channel the decoder currently
follows is marked with a greater-than sign and kept in view. The right pane
shows decoded messages prefixed with stream time, signed frequency and channel
id, wrapped to the pane width. A message that keeps growing updates its own
line until another channel decodes something.
.PP
.B q
or
.B ESC
quits,
.B c
clears the message pane,
.B Up
and
.B Down
scroll one line,
.B PgUp
and
.B PgDn
scroll one screen,
.B Home
jumps to the oldest retained line and
.B End
returns to the live end. The footer shows the retained line count and whether
the view is scrolled back.
.PP
The view takes over the terminal, not standard input, which keeps carrying the
I/Q pairs. On Windows it attaches to the console, so it needs a real console
rather than a pipe. Text snapshots are suppressed while the view is active, but
.B -j
and
.B -t
still write to standard output and to their file. Terminal support comes from
ncurses and is enabled by default; configure with
.B -Dtui=disabled
to build the tools without it.
.SH EXAMPLES
Decode 48 kHz signed 16-bit I/Q, centered at zero IF:
.PP
.nf
morsedec-iqlive -r 48000 -f 0 -j < capture.iq
.fi
.PP
Decode float I/Q while retaining offsets from -12 kHz through 12 kHz:
.PP
.nf
morsedec-iqlive -F f32 -r 48000 -l -12000 -u 12000 -a < capture.iq
.fi
.PP
Follow a live I/Q stream in the terminal view:
.PP
.nf
sdr-stream | morsedec-iqlive -F f32 -r 48000 -l -12000 -u 12000 -T
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 after successful decoding and output, or a nonzero status if input,
configuration, decoding, or output fails.
